    'body': """A political party is a voluntary and organised association or group of individuals who share broadly similar political ideas, values and policy goals, and who come together with the express purpose of winning political power, whether by contesting and winning elections or by other constitutional means, in order to gain control of government and to implement their preferred policies and programmes for the governance of the state. Political parties serve as one of the most essential institutions linking ordinary citizens to the formal machinery of government within any democratic system.

Political parties perform several important functions within a democratic political system. They aggregate and articulate the diverse interests, concerns and demands of different groups within society, translating these into coherent policy programmes and manifestos that voters can meaningfully evaluate and choose between. They recruit, select and present candidates for election to public office at various levels of government, from local councils up to the presidency. They mobilise and educate the electorate, encouraging citizen participation in the political process and helping voters understand the choices and issues at stake in any given election. They provide a structured and constitutional means through which political opposition to the government of the day can be organised and expressed, rather than opposition manifesting through unconstitutional or violent means. Finally, once in power, the winning party or coalition of parties is responsible for forming the government and translating its electoral promises and manifesto commitments into actual public policy.

Party systems, meaning the overall pattern and number of parties that meaningfully compete for and are capable of winning political power within a given state, are generally classified into three broad types. A one-party system is one in which only a single political party is legally permitted to exist and to contest for political power, with all other parties being banned, restricted, or otherwise unable to genuinely compete, a system historically associated with several African and communist states, and generally regarded as less democratic since it denies citizens a genuine electoral choice between competing parties and programmes. A two-party system is one in which, although more than one party may legally exist, political power realistically alternates between only two major, dominant parties, with any smaller or minor parties generally unable to seriously compete for outright control of government, a system closely associated with the United States (Democratic and Republican parties) and, to a significant degree, the United Kingdom (Labour and Conservative parties). A multi-party system is one in which several parties of genuinely significant size and electoral strength exist and compete meaningfully for political power, often meaning that no single party is able to win an outright majority on its own, thereby frequently necessitating the formation of coalition governments among two or more parties in order to command a working majority; Nigeria's Fourth Republic has generally operated as a multi-party system, albeit with certain periods of practical dominance by one or two particularly large parties.

Political parties are typically expected to be built around, and to campaign upon, a broadly coherent set of political ideas, principles and policy positions, often described as the party's ideology or manifesto, which is intended to distinguish it from rival parties and to give the electorate a genuine and meaningful basis upon which to make an informed choice between competing visions for how the state should be governed, although in many developing democracies, including Nigeria, political parties are sometimes criticised for being organised more around the personal ambitions and patronage networks of prominent individual politicians than around genuinely distinct and coherent ideological programmes.

Sample Exam-Style Questions:
1. Question: Define a political party and state two of its main functions. Answer: A political party is a voluntary, organised association of individuals sharing similar political ideas who come together to seek and win political power through constitutional means such as elections. Two of its main functions are recruiting and presenting candidates for public office, and aggregating diverse public interests into coherent policy programmes that voters can evaluate.
2. Question: Distinguish between a two-party system and a multi-party system, giving one example of each. Answer: In a two-party system, political power realistically alternates between only two dominant parties, as in the United States with its Democratic and Republican parties. In a multi-party system, several parties of significant electoral strength compete meaningfully for power, often necessitating coalition governments, as has generally been the case in Nigeria's Fourth Republic.
3. Question: Explain why a one-party system is generally regarded as less democratic than a multi-party system. Answer: A one-party system denies citizens any genuine choice between rival parties and competing policy programmes, since only one party is legally permitted to contest for power, which removes meaningful electoral accountability and makes it difficult for citizens to remove an unpopular or ineffective government through the ballot box, a central feature of genuine democratic practice.
4. Question: Toluwani argues that Nigerian political parties are usually built around strong ideological differences, similar to parties in many Western democracies. Evaluate this claim. Answer: Toluwani's claim is generally considered inaccurate by most observers of Nigerian politics. Nigerian political parties are frequently criticised for lacking clearly distinct ideological platforms, and are often observed to be organised more around the personal followings, ambitions and patronage networks of prominent politicians, with candidates sometimes moving between parties without significant ideological conflict, rather than around genuinely coherent and differentiated policy programmes.

